# Mobile overlays render in the tree

Every dialog, sheet, menu, and viewer that appears **over app content** mounts through
**`AppOverlay`** from [`src/components/overlay`](/apps/mobile/src/components/overlay/index.ts). It
registers its children with the overlay host, which renders them from `OverlayOutlet` above the
navigator — one React tree, one native window.

```tsx
// GOOD
<AppOverlay onRequestClose={onCancel} visible={visible}>
  <Pressable onPress={onCancel} style={styles.backdrop}>…</Pressable>
</AppOverlay>
```

`AppOverlay` carries the parts an overlay needs, so no caller reimplements them: `OverlayA11yShield`
hides the app beneath from VoiceOver and TalkBack while an overlay is open, Android's hardware back
routes to `onRequestClose`, overlays stack in the order they opened, and the host is subscribed
through `useSyncExternalStore` so opening one does not re-render the app.

## Why not `Modal`

A React Native `Modal` creates a separate native window. On Android, `ReactModalHostView` posts that
window's size to the shadow tree as an **asynchronous state update**, and the modal's subtree has no
size until it lands. When a `react-native-screens` **root-stack card** covers the tabs — the full
player, make clip, V4V — that update never arrives.

The failure is quiet and total. The subtree stays 0×0, so the overlay paints as a small stamp in the
top-left corner **and is excluded from the Android accessibility tree entirely**: TalkBack cannot
reach it, and neither can Maestro. Nothing redboxes, and the press that opened it did fire. Sizing
the content from `useWindowDimensions()` fixes the painting and leaves the accessibility hole, which
is why the overlay must not live in a second window at all.

## Do

- Reach for `AppOverlay` for every overlay over content: confirm dialogs, action sheets, overflow
  menus, image viewers, managed-copy readers, rate-limit notices.
- Render `AppOverlay` unconditionally and let `visible` drive it, the same contract as
  [mobile-modal-presentation-lifecycle](/.cursor/rules/mobile-modal-presentation-lifecycle.mdc).
- Animate with `Animated` inside the overlay's own children — the host does not animate for you.
- Give the overlay's content `accessibilityViewIsModal` and a role, so assistive tech treats it as
  the focused surface.

## Don't

- Do not import `Modal` in `apps/mobile` for an overlay over content, even one that "works on iOS".
- Do not work around a clipped or unreachable overlay by measuring the window, raising `zIndex`, or
  setting `statusBarTranslucent`. Those address the paint, not the accessibility tree.
- Do not mount an overlay outside `OverlayHostProvider`; `AppOverlay` throws rather than render into
  nowhere.

## Flows see through an open overlay

One window means the covered screen's `testID`s stay in the hierarchy while an overlay is open.
`OverlayA11yShield` marks that subtree unimportant for accessibility, which is what TalkBack and
VoiceOver honor — but UiAutomator asks for unimportant views too, so **Maestro still finds and taps
them**. A `Modal` used to hide them for free; nothing does now.

So in a Maestro flow, `assertVisible` on a screen underneath proves nothing, and a tap aimed at it
lands on whatever the overlay put at those coordinates. Header buttons are the trap: a screen's back
button and an overlay's close button occupy the same corner, so the tap "passes" and the flow reads
as a dead control.

Dismiss the overlay explicitly, then interact with the screen:

```yaml
# GOOD — the once-only how-to covers the form; close it before touching the form's header
- tapOn:
    id: full-player-create-clip
- extendedWaitUntil:
    visible:
      id: make-clip-how-to-modal
    timeout: ${TIMEOUT_SLOW}
- tapOn:
    id: make-clip-how-to-close
- assertNotVisible:
    id: make-clip-how-to-modal
- assertVisible:
    id: make-clip-screen
```

## The one boundary

`App.tsx`'s auth sheet and the popularity-tracking consent prompt stay RN `Modal`s with
`presentationStyle="fullScreen"`. They are top-level app modes that replace the whole screen, they
only ever appear over the tab navigator, and a presented view controller is the platform-correct
presentation for them.

That boundary is narrow: a full-screen mode at the root, never over a root-stack card. Anything that
can open while the full player or make clip is up belongs in `AppOverlay`, and new overlays start
there.

`OverlayOutlet` mounts inside `AppBody`. The login and signup sheet in `App.tsx` is a native `Modal`
with `presentationStyle="fullScreen"` and a **sibling** of the outlet. Any `ConfirmDialog`,
`MoreMenu`, or managed-copy sheet raised from login or signup therefore renders underneath the auth
window: present in the tree, reachable by tests, invisible to the user. Keep auth-screen messaging
inline, or move the auth sheet into the overlay host first. Do not raise an `AppOverlay` from a
surface that is still an RN `Modal`.

